  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT MON OCT 5 2026\n\nRain will continue to clear the region this evening as the cold\nfront tracks southward. The cold front will quickly retrograde\nnorthward on Tuesday, allowing high temperatures to rebound into\nthe low to mid-80s. Meanwhile, cooler air filtering around the\nsouthern Appalachians will keep overnight lows pleasantly\nseasonable into Friday. However, we should remain dry for much of\nthe week. Tweaked guidance a few degrees cooler in our normally\ncolder valleys for the next several nights.\n\nThis steady pattern will persist through most of the workweek\nbefore breaking down heading into the weekend. Long-range\nguidance consistently points toward a synoptic pattern shift by\nFriday, though models vary on intensity, they consistently\nindicate a developing system. A weak low-pressure system is\nexpected to form within the Bay of Campeche and interact with\nseveral shortwaves over the Gulf. This setup will draw in Gulf\nmoisture and bring increasing rain chances as the energy moves\ninland. The latest National Hurricane Center forecast puts a 60% \nchance of tropical cyclone development over the next 48 hours \n(through Wednesday), and 80% chance of development within the \nnext 7 days. While confidence is high that the current pattern \nwill break down, confidence in the specific details remains low \ndue to significant model variance and timing.\n\n16",
